    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so it's mostly just about recovery and managing fatigue. i guess my concern is that sometimes it feels like there's something else going on, like the progress just stops for no reason at all despite everything being on paper. how would you explain that \"stagnant\" feeling from a mechanical or neurological level? like why does the body just... refuse to move forward sometimes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That \"stagnant\" feeling, when all the training variables look perfect, often points to a neurological bottleneck, which is how the brain tells the muscles to fire. Imagine your nervous system is like a poorly maintained network; if it's constantly overloaded, it starts to throttle the signal to the muscles, even if the muscles themselves are ready. This is a common issue when training too intensely for too long, leading to central nervous system fatigue. The body isn't refusing to move forward; it's temporarily unable to *command* the movement efficiently because the signaling pathways are fatigued. A deload helps \"re-calibrate\" that nervous system, allowing for more potent motor unit recruitment.",
      "source": "target",
