    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "right, so it's more like a communication error between the brain and the legs. it's just hard to shake the feeling that it's almost like an invisible wall. sometimes it feels less like a biological limit and more like some weird, irrational force is actively working against the athlete, you know? like they're being held back by something they can't see. do you think there's any psychological component to that \"invisible\" resistance, or is it strictly just the CNS issues you mentioned?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"invisible wall\" feeling is very real, and it often has a significant psychological component, even when the root cause is physiological. When an athlete hits a plateau, the mental narrative can become self-defeating, creating a feedback loop of perceived failure. This psychological resistance, or performance anxiety around the lift, can manifest as a genuine inability to recruit maximum force, which feels exactly like an external, irrational force holding them back. It's a cognitive load layered on top of the CNS fatigue.\n\nHowever, it’s not *just* mental; the mind and body are deeply intertwined, which is why the CNS issues feel so psychological. A lack of confidence, for instance, can trigger protective muscle bracing patterns that are inefficient, essentially creating a self-imposed mechanical limitation. A successful deload, combined with positive reinforcement, helps break this cycle by proving to the athlete that progress *is* possible again.",
      "source": "target",
